## Proof

We want to show that if $(i(x_n))_n$ and $(i(y_n))_n$ are two sequences in $i[X]$ both converging to the same limit $p \in X'$, and if $(f(x_n))_n$ converges to $q \in Y$, then $(f(y_n))_n$ also converges to $q$.

**Key idea.** Since both sequences converge to the same point in $X'$, they must be "asymptotically close" in $X'$, hence (by isometry of $i$) asymptotically close in $X$, hence (by isometry of $f$) asymptotically close in $Y$. Combined with $f(x_n) \to q$, this forces $f(y_n) \to q$.

**Detailed argument.**

Since $i(x_n) \to p$ and $i(y_n) \to p$ in $X'$, by the triangle inequality in $X'$:

$$d_{X'}\bigl(i(x_n),\, i(y_n)\bigr) \le d_{X'}\bigl(i(x_n),\, p\bigr) + d_{X'}\bigl(p,\, i(y_n)\bigr) \to 0 + 0 = 0.$$

Hence

$$d_{X'}\bigl(i(x_n),\, i(y_n)\bigr) \to 0.$$

Since $i : X \to X'$ is an isometry, it preserves distances, so

$$d_X(x_n,\, y_n) = d_{X'}\bigl(i(x_n),\, i(y_n)\bigr) \to 0.$$

Since $f : X \to Y$ is an isometry, it likewise preserves distances, so

$$d_Y\bigl(f(x_n),\, f(y_n)\bigr) = d_X(x_n,\, y_n) \to 0.$$

Now we use the convergence $f(x_n) \to q$ in $Y$. By the triangle inequality in $Y$:

$$d_Y\bigl(f(y_n),\, q\bigr) \le d_Y\bigl(f(y_n),\, f(x_n)\bigr) + d_Y\bigl(f(x_n),\, q\bigr).$$

Both terms on the right-hand side tend to $0$: the first by the result above, the second by the hypothesis $f(x_n) \to q$. Therefore

$$d_Y\bigl(f(y_n),\, q\bigr) \to 0,$$

which is precisely $f(y_n) \to q$ in $Y$. $\blacksquare$

**Conclusion.** Yes, $(f(y_n))_n$ also converges to $q$ in $Y$. The limit depends only on the point $p \in X'$, not on the particular sequence in $i[X]$ used to approach $p$. This is exactly the well-definedness needed to construct a canonical isometry $X' \to Y$ extending $f \circ i^{-1}$, establishing the uniqueness (up to isometry) of the metric completion.

